.TH New-DockerNetwork 1 "December 2025" "Noble Factor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
New-DockerNetwork \- create a Docker network using Noble Factor conventions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B New-DockerNetwork
\fI[--device <name>] [--driver <bridge|ipvlan|macvlan>] [--ip-range <CIDR>] [--help]\fR <network-name>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Create a Docker network following Noble Factor conventions.
The command discovers host networking information, chooses sensible defaults per operating system, and
creates a user-defined Docker network. On Linux, when an IP range is provided for \fImacvlan\fR or \fIipvlan\fR,
the command also creates a host "shim" link (macvlan in bridge mode) so the host can reach containers on the
new network.
.PP
This manual documents the behaviour implemented by the script installed at
\fBbuild/New-DockerNetwork\fR.
.SS Discovery and defaults
On macOS the script discovers the device IPv4 via \fBifconfig\fR and system DNS data. On Linux it prefers NetworkManager
queries (\fBnmcli\fR) and the system default route. If no device is provided the script sets a default per-OS.

.SS Driver-specific rules
.PP
- \fIbridge\fR: Creates a bridge user network using the discovered IPv4 subnet and gateway.
- \fImacvlan\fR / \fIipvlan\fR: Require \fI--ip-range\fR; the script parses the range using \fIipcalc\fR to derive an
  IPv4 host address to reserve for the host shim and a container address pool. The parent device is specified via
  the \fB--opt parent=...\fR option to \fBdocker network create\fR.

.SS IPv6 handling (Linux)
On Linux the script detects an IPv6 route prefix and, if present, passes \fB--ipv6\fR to \fBdocker network create\fR.
When an explicit IPv6 subnet is not supplied, Docker will allocate an IPv6 ULA (Unique Local Address) prefix for the
network and containers will be assigned IPv6 addresses at container creation time. The script also writes udev rules
and sysctl commands to harden the created shim interface against IPv6 autoconfiguration and unwanted ND behaviour
so the host does not inadvertently acquire or advertise IPv6 addresses on the shim.
.PP
Note: IPv6 handling in the script is implemented for Linux only; macOS does not currently support Docker-managed
IPv6 networks and therefore is not configured for IPv6 by this script.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --device <name>
Parent network device to use when creating macvlan/ipvlan networks. If omitted a sensible default is selected
depending on the operating system (macOS: derived from system DNS info; Linux: default route device).
.TP
.B -d, --driver <bridge|ipvlan|macvlan>
Network driver to create. Default: \fIbridge\fR on macOS, \fImacvlan\fR on Linux. The script supports \fIbridge\fR,
\fImacvlan\fR and \fIipvlan\fR. \fIhost\fR and \fInone\fR are recognised but result in minimal/no changes.
.TP
.B --ip-range <CIDR>
IPv4 CIDR that constrains addresses for container assignment. Required for \fImacvlan\fR and \fIipvlan\fR drivers; not
applicable (ignored) for \fIbridge\fR, \fIhost\fR or \fInone\fR. The script uses \fIipcalc\fR to parse IPv4 ranges.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show short usage information.
.SH SHIM LINK (Linux only)
When an \fI--ip-range\fR is provided and the platform is Linux, the script creates a host shim link named
"\fI<network-name>_shim\fR" (first 10 characters of network name plus \fB_shim\fR suffix) as a macvlan device in bridge mode attached to the parent device. The shim:

.IP
.TP 0.5i
- receives a reserved IPv4 address from the parsed range via \fBip addr add\fR
.TP 0.5i
- is set up and brought up with \fBip link set ... up\fR
.TP 0.5i
- has a route added for the new IPv4 range via \fBip route add\fR so the host can reach containers
.EI

To reduce multicast/ARP/ND conflicts the script:

.IP
.TP 0.5i
- updates \fB/etc/avahi/avahi-daemon.conf\fR to add the shim to \fIdeny-interfaces\fR so avahi-daemon ignores mDNS on the shim
.TP 0.5i
- sets IPv4 sysctls on the shim: \fBnet.ipv4.conf.<shim>.arp_ignore=1\fR and \fBnet.ipv4.conf.<shim>.arp_announce=2\fR
.TP 0.5i
- writes IPv6-related sysctls to disable RA/autoconf/IPv6 on the shim and persists them via a udev rules file
  under \fB/etc/udev/rules.d/99-<shim>.rules\fR (for example: \fBnet.ipv6.conf.<shim>.accept_ra=0\fR,
  \fBnet.ipv6.conf.<shim>.autoconf=0\fR, \fBnet.ipv6.conf.<shim>.disable_ipv6=1\fR)
.EI

.SH PRIVILEGES AND DEPENDENCIES
The script requires elevated privileges (calls are run via \fBsudo\fR). It also assumes the presence of these utilities:

.IP
.TP 0.5i
- \fBdocker\fR
- \fBip\fR (iproute2)
- \fBipcalc\fR (IPv4 parsing)
- \fBnmcli\fR (NetworkManager queries on Linux)
- \fBifconfig\fR / \fBscutil\fR (macOS discovery)
- \fBsed\fR (GNU sed required on macOS)
.EI

If these tools are absent the script will fail; the script reports errors through an \fBerror\fR helper defined in its
bootstrap (\fBDeclare-BashScript\fR).
.SH FILES
.TP
/etc/avahi/avahi-daemon.conf
.br
/etc/udev/rules.d/99-<shim>.rules
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Create a macvlan network on Linux named \fBhomebridge_eth0\fR using device \fBeth0\fR:
.RS
.nf
  sudo New-DockerNetwork --device eth0 --driver macvlan --ip-range 192.168.50.0/24 homebridge_eth0
.fi
.RE

Create a bridge network on macOS named \fBhomebridge_en0\fR (device autodetected):
.RS
.nf
  New-DockerNetwork --driver bridge homebridge_en0
.fi
.RE
.SH OUTPUT
On success, the script prints the Docker network ID to stdout. This can be captured for use by other scripts
or for cleanup via \fBRemove-DockerNetwork\fR.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The script prints diagnostic output via \fBdocker network inspect\fR and shows link state on Linux (\fBip -s link show\fR)
when networks or shim links are created.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success
.TP
1
Error (missing dependencies, network creation failure)
.TP
2
Invalid arguments or unsupported OS
.SH SEE ALSO
Remove-DockerNetwork(1), docker-network-create(1), ip(8), ipcalc(8), nmcli(1), avahi-daemon(8)
.SH AUTHOR
Noble Factor
